So here's the deal: it's not possible to get the exact item that just broke, but it's possible to work around this to an extent.

Armors can only be worn one to a slot, so we just need to check if the broken item was a helmet, chestplate, etc, and break the armor piece in that slot.
For other tools, we can test against individual tools being broken.

An important observation is that even if you hold two of the same tool at a time, only the one in the mainhand can take durability damage.
A tool in the offhand can only take damage if there is a tool in the mainhand without applicable right-click functionality. If the tools are the same,
they both have the same right-click functionality. Therefore, we test if a matching ID is in the mainhand, and if it is, break uit and return. If it isn't,
test the offhand. If it's not in the offhand, something's gone terribly wrong.

This won't work with modded items, since every item has to be hard-coded in this way. So for items not in a tag, we could still have a generic method of testing.
Or, we could not worry about modded items.